   "title": "Read me",
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## 15. Location walkthrough\nMakes a **location RefCap from a video walking through a place**: four frames spread evenly over the walk become the views. A test shot then puts the character in the room. One queue runs it all.\n\n**Steps**\n1. Load the walk in **Your clip**: no people, a slow steady move through one room, 24 fps, 720p at most. Set the **Trim** to the 5.2 s that show the most of the room.\n2. Set `name` on the build node and queue. The views are taken at the clip's first frame, one third, two thirds and the last frame. They carry no camera heading, so their yaws are all 0; pick them by number.\n3. In the prompt, name the room as a subject (`<Subject 2> is the room in <Picture 6>, <Picture 7>, <Picture 8> and <Picture 9>, ...`). Say which part the camera faces, and which picture shows it (`...faces the white wardrobe beside the window, as in <Picture 6>`).\n\n**Why views and not the clip** (three Pexels walkthroughs, lf_013, 2 shots x 2 seeds; REFCAP_README.md, \"Video references\"):\n- Any reference kept the room far better than words: CLIP-H similarity to the source room 0.78-0.82 against 0.66 with the room described in words, where a different room scores 0.47.\n- The four views beat the whole clip as `<Video 1>` in 10 of 12 pairs (+0.02), at 1,816 reference tokens and 65 s per clip against 8,713 and 101 s. The clip at 320 px was no worse than the full one. Two views plus the clip tied the four views.\n- The clip arms faced the requested part of the room less reliably.\n\n**Using it:** like workflow 9's RefCaps, after the character's in a pack (workflow 7). At most 9 pictures per shot, so 5 face views + these 4.\n\n**Settings in these workflows** follow ComfyUI's MiniMax H3 reference-to-video template: the ref2va model with its 4-step Lightning LoRA, 4 steps, `res_multistep` + `simple`, 608x352. For the base model, bypass the *Lightning LoRA* node and use 20 steps. The measurements quoted here ran on a community H3 turbo finetune (shift 6/3, 8 steps) on a 12 GB GPU. Raise the size and length for final renders; the reference tokens ride through every step, so more photos and larger captures cost time."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
